## Why This Is Asked
IBM wants to see how you handle change management, influence without authority, and drive technical decisions through data and collaboration rather than just technical expertise.

## Expected Answer
A strong candidate will describe: 1) A specific technical challenge or opportunity, 2) How they gathered data/evidence to support their proposal, 3) How they addressed team concerns through demos or proof-of-concepts, 4) The measurable business impact of the change.

## Code Example
```typescript
// Example: Converting team to TypeScript
interface LegacyAPI {
  data: any;
  status: string;
}

// Improved TypeScript version
interface ModernAPI<T> {
  data: T;
  status: 'success' | 'error' | 'pending';
}

// Proof of concept showing type safety
function processAPI<T>(response: ModernAPI<T>): T | null {
  return response.status === 'success' ? response.data : null;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question assesses incident leadership skills—specifically the ability to make quick, data-driven decisions under pressure while balancing product velocity with system reliability. It tests understanding of rollback versus patch tradeoffs and stakeholder management during high-stress situations.

## Key Concepts

- Ownership and accountability in incident response
- Data-driven decision making under pressure
- Stakeholder alignment and clear communication
- Risk assessment: rollback vs. patch tradeoffs

## Code Example

```javascript
function triage(telemetry) {
  const scope = assessScope(telemetry);
  const rootCause = identifyRootCause(scope);

  if (isLowRiskRollback()) {
    executeRollback();
  } else {
    deployKillSwitch();
  }

  communicateStakeholders(resolution);
  return monitorRecovery();
}
```
